WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.000
Conversion of Probabilities to the Sigma Scale

2
00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:10.933
Welcome to this session on establishing baselines. We oftentimes get the question,

3
00:00:10.933 --> 00:00:14.566
how do we convert from a probability to a Sigma?

4
00:00:14.566 --> 00:00:17.532
Where do the PPM's come from, and all that?

5
00:00:17.533 --> 00:00:20.899
Well, we can make these calculations in Excel.

6
00:00:20.900 --> 00:00:27.566
For example, if I want to convert from a Sigma to a probability, I can put in the Sigma

7
00:00:27.566 --> 00:00:33.166
and the probability I get back is a one tailed probability.

8
00:00:33.166 --> 00:00:37.832
In other words, it means it's equivalent to a unilateral specification.

9
00:00:37.833 --> 00:00:43.233
So, if I had a unilateral specification with a

10
00:00:43.233 --> 00:00:46.333
short-term capability of Three Sigma,

11
00:00:46.333 --> 00:00:52.666
That's the 66,807 ppm defective.

12
00:00:52.666 --> 00:00:59.266
Notice, that I took into account the shift. I subtracted 1.5 from 3.

13
00:00:59.266 --> 00:01:05.732
Sigma is always short-term, and defects per million opportunities.

14
00:01:05.733 --> 00:01:11.666
DPMO here, is always long-term.

15
00:01:11.666 --> 00:01:18.632
So, I account for that in my equation by simply subtracting 1.5.

16
00:01:18.633 --> 00:01:22.333
And that's how I create that baseline if you will.

17
00:01:22.333 --> 00:01:27.333
I can also convert defect to Sigma.

18
00:01:27.333 --> 00:01:31.133
I go out measure the opportunities I got,

19
00:01:31.133 --> 00:01:34.733
count the defects get my defects per million opportunities.

20
00:01:34.733 --> 00:01:39.899
Come into handy-dandy Excel, make this calculation, and I can convert

21
00:01:39.900 --> 00:01:44.633
probability back into to Sigma. DPMO back into Sigma.

22
00:01:44.633 --> 00:01:48.066
Fairly simple and straight forward process.

23
00:01:48.066 --> 00:01:53.066
Thank you for choosing SSMI.
